Why picolibc's copies
---------------------

newlib's own rx/memcpy.S, memmove.S and memset.S are the same code, with
byte loops in memcpy and memmove for builds that disallow the string
instructions, but they carry no notice at all, and newlib's COPYING.NEWLIB doesn't say which of its
licences applies to a file without one. picolibc names the licence.


What was looked at
------------------

memcmp   Comes from libterae's own C, in src/port/generic, which
         port_memcmp.c includes. No memcmp in assembly for RX exists in
         newlib, picolibc or NuttX, whose rx folder has only setjmp.


What is checked
---------------

clang has no RX target, and no build of GNU binutils for it is published
for macOS, so binutils 2.47 is built for rx-elf from its source. The check
runs port_memcpy.S, port_memmove.S and port_memset.S through clang's
preprocessor with none of its own macros, given what GCC 16.1 defines for
each processor in its config/rx/rx.h, and assembles them with that GNU as
for the RX100, the RX200, the RX600 with little-endian and big-endian
data, and the RX610. It checks that they define only the names C reserves,
with the _ that RX puts in front of C names, and leave nothing undefined.
GCC defines __RX_ALLOW_STRING_INSNS__ unless -mno-allow-string-insns is
given, and without it each wrapper stops with its error, as it says it
does.

port_memcmp.c is not compiled here, as nothing here compiles C for RX.
Nothing in this folder has run: before it is relied on, it needs a build
with rx-elf-gcc and a run on hardware or an emulator, such as QEMU's
gdbsim-r5f562n8 board, an RX62N.


